  "download_disabled": false,
  "description": "This study examines a <em>pre-modern manuscript</em> containing botanical illustrations, looping glyph structures, and non-linear textual encoding. Through statistical glyph-frequency modeling, recursive pattern analysis, and comparative semiotic mapping, the research identifies structural features consistent with <strong>constructed languages</strong> rather than natural linguistic evolution. The manuscript's symbolic clusters exhibit fractal-like repetition, suggesting intentional encoding designed to obscure semantic anchors. Although several glyph families correlate loosely with known medieval cipher traditions, the overall system remains resistant to conventional decryption. The study proposes a multi-layered encoding hypothesis involving visual-semantic coupling, non-linear reading order, and context-dependent glyph transformation.",
